to overturn the 2020 election. It reads just like&nbsp;
the Georgia story, but it's a different story. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.480
a phone call in late 2020, Trump tried to pressure&nbsp;
Republican Arizona Governor Doug Ducey to overturn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.700
the state's presidential election results, saying&nbsp;
that if enough fraudulent votes could be found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.620
it would overcome Trump's narrow loss in Arizona,&nbsp;
according to three people familiar with the call.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:35.460
Trump also repeatedly asked Vice President Mike&nbsp;
Pence to call Ducey and prod him to find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.500
evidence to substantiate Trump's claims of fraud.&nbsp;
Pence called Ducey several times to discuss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.900
election. They said, though he did not follow&nbsp;
Trump's directions to pressure the governor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:52.440
the extent of Trump's efforts to cajole Ducey&nbsp;
into helping him stay in power has not before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:58.020
been reported. Even as Trump's other efforts&nbsp;
have been made public. Ducey told reporters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.040
in December 2020 that he and Trump had spoken,&nbsp;
but declined to disclose the contents of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.120
call. Although he disagreed with Trump about&nbsp;
the outcome of the election, Ducey has sought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:12.000
to avoid a public battle from Trump, and notably,&nbsp;
Doocy did not record the call, according to people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.440
familiar with the matter. So we now have. Yet&nbsp;
another story we have now heard. If you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:26.340
nationally from county election officials,&nbsp;
state election officials, state legislators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

